1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,750
You're looking at 27 British ships charging

2
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:05,010
33 French and Spanish warships,

3
00:00:05,010 --> 00:00:07,920
in 1805. By sunset,

4
00:00:08,430 --> 00:00:09,800
Britain had lost none.

5
00:00:10,370 --> 00:00:11,500
What did Nelson know?

6
00:00:12,130 --> 00:00:16,230
October 21, 1805. Off Cape Trafalgar,

7
00:00:16,230 --> 00:00:17,960
on the southern coast of Spain.

8
00:00:18,616 --> 00:00:20,936
This is the Battle of Trafalgar.

9
00:00:20,936 --> 00:00:23,126
What happened, and why it happened at all.

10
00:00:24,041 --> 00:00:26,431
Two years earlier, Europe is at war again.

11
00:00:27,581 --> 00:00:29,361
Napoleon Bonaparte commands the strongest

12
00:00:29,361 --> 00:00:30,891
army on the continent.

13
00:00:31,621 --> 00:00:34,231
Only one thing stands between him and Britain:

14
00:00:34,661 --> 00:00:35,611
the Royal Navy.

15
00:00:36,313 --> 00:00:38,183
At Boulogne, on the French coast,

16
00:00:38,613 --> 00:00:41,393
Napoleon gathers an invasion army and a

17
00:00:41,393 --> 00:00:43,563
fleet of flat-bottomed barges.

18
00:00:44,193 --> 00:00:46,803
All he needs is control of the English Channel.

19
00:00:47,367 --> 00:00:49,347
But British warships blockade the French

20
00:00:49,347 --> 00:00:51,807
ports. The French fleets are trapped,

21
00:00:51,807 --> 00:00:53,637
at Toulon and at Brest.

22
00:00:54,301 --> 00:00:56,091
So Napoleon makes a plan.

23
00:00:56,821 --> 00:00:58,171
His fleets will slip out,

24
00:00:58,301 --> 00:00:59,971
cross the Atlantic to the Caribbean,

25
00:01:00,721 --> 00:01:01,731
lure the British away,

26
00:01:02,241 --> 00:01:04,361
then race back together and sweep the Channel

27
00:01:04,361 --> 00:01:04,851
clear.

28
00:01:05,453 --> 00:01:09,140
The man given that job is Vice-Admiral Pierre-Charles

29
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:11,483
Villeneuve. Seven years earlier,

30
00:01:11,733 --> 00:01:13,853
he escaped when Nelson destroyed a French

31
00:01:13,853 --> 00:01:14,963
fleet at the Nile.

32
00:01:15,633 --> 00:01:17,243
He has no wish to face him again.

33
00:01:17,957 --> 00:01:19,517
In the spring of 1805,

34
00:01:19,517 --> 00:01:22,437
Villeneuve slips out of Toulon and crosses

35
00:01:22,437 --> 00:01:25,797
the Atlantic. Nelson chases him to the West

36
00:01:25,797 --> 00:01:27,127
Indies, and back.

37
00:01:27,709 --> 00:01:30,389
Off northern Spain, a British squadron catches

38
00:01:30,389 --> 00:01:33,779
the combined fleet. The fight is inconclusive.

39
00:01:34,425 --> 00:01:36,365
Napoleon orders Villeneuve north,

40
00:01:36,365 --> 00:01:39,465
to Brest. Instead, afraid the British are

41
00:01:39,465 --> 00:01:41,105
watching, he turns south,

42
00:01:41,105 --> 00:01:43,367
to the Spanish port of Cádiz.

43
00:01:43,637 --> 00:01:46,377
At Boulogne, Napoleon's army waits for a

44
00:01:46,377 --> 00:01:47,647
fleet that never comes.

45
00:01:48,497 --> 00:01:50,537
On August 25, it marches away,

46
00:01:50,537 --> 00:01:52,187
east, to fight Austria.

47
00:01:52,937 --> 00:01:54,687
The invasion of Britain is off.

48
00:01:55,359 --> 00:01:58,299
Remember that. Trafalgar did not stop the

49
00:01:58,299 --> 00:02:00,529
invasion. It was already cancelled.

50
00:02:01,159 --> 00:02:03,529
What Trafalgar decided was something bigger.

51
00:02:04,392 --> 00:02:06,852
Nelson takes command off Cádiz at the end

52
00:02:06,852 --> 00:02:09,712
of September. He keeps his main fleet about

53
00:02:09,712 --> 00:02:11,232
50 miles out to sea,

54
00:02:11,232 --> 00:02:13,912
out of sight, with fast frigates watching

55
00:02:13,912 --> 00:02:14,937
the harbour.

56
00:02:15,207 --> 00:02:17,697
Inside, the combined fleet is struggling.

57
00:02:18,027 --> 00:02:21,227
Supplies are short. The ships are more than

58
00:02:21,227 --> 00:02:23,367
2,000 men short of full crews,

59
00:02:23,367 --> 00:02:26,401
and many sailors have barely been to sea.

60
00:02:26,671 --> 00:02:28,951
Then Napoleon orders Villeneuve to sail

61
00:02:28,951 --> 00:02:31,584
for Naples. And on October 18,

62
00:02:31,584 --> 00:02:34,771
Villeneuve learns something worse: another

63
00:02:34,771 --> 00:02:37,341
admiral is on his way to replace him.

64
00:02:37,799 --> 00:02:39,269
Rather than be disgraced,

65
00:02:39,319 --> 00:02:40,129
he puts to sea.

66
00:02:40,899 --> 00:02:44,039
On October 20, 33 ships of the line leave

67
00:02:44,039 --> 00:02:46,149
Cádiz. Only days earlier,

68
00:02:46,399 --> 00:02:48,809
his own captains had voted to stay in port.

69
00:02:49,672 --> 00:02:51,812
In those days, fleets fought in two long

70
00:02:51,812 --> 00:02:54,122
lines, side by side,

71
00:02:54,152 --> 00:02:56,482
trading broadsides until one gave up.

72
00:02:56,952 --> 00:02:58,882
It was orderly. It was safe.

73
00:02:59,372 --> 00:03:01,162
And it rarely destroyed anyone.

74
00:03:01,742 --> 00:03:03,492
Nelson had a different idea.

75
00:03:04,182 --> 00:03:05,582
Over dinners aboard his flagship,

76
00:03:05,582 --> 00:03:09,392
HMS Victory, he explained it to his captains.

77
00:03:10,026 --> 00:03:12,546
His fleet would form two columns and sail

78
00:03:12,546 --> 00:03:13,986
straight at the enemy line,

79
00:03:13,986 --> 00:03:15,636
at a right angle.

80
00:03:16,166 --> 00:03:18,196
They would break through it in two places,

81
00:03:18,566 --> 00:03:19,976
cut it into three pieces,

82
00:03:20,206 --> 00:03:22,246
and crush the centre and rear before the

83
00:03:22,246 --> 00:03:23,836
front could turn back to help.

84
00:03:24,425 --> 00:03:25,575
The risk was huge.

85
00:03:26,445 --> 00:03:28,425
Sailing head-on, the leading British ships

86
00:03:28,425 --> 00:03:29,715
could not fire back.

87
00:03:30,165 --> 00:03:31,175
For a long time,

88
00:03:31,205 --> 00:03:32,255
they would just be targets.

89
00:03:33,148 --> 00:03:35,308
Villeneuve guessed Nelson might try exactly

90
00:03:35,308 --> 00:03:38,148
this. But his crews were too inexperienced

91
00:03:38,148 --> 00:03:41,028
for anything clever. He kept the single

92
00:03:41,028 --> 00:03:41,698
line.

93
00:03:42,512 --> 00:03:45,842
Dawn, October 21. The wind is light.

94
00:03:46,472 --> 00:03:48,502
From Victory, the British can see the enemy:

95
00:03:48,772 --> 00:03:49,992
a long line of sails,

96
00:03:49,992 --> 00:03:51,282
stretching for miles.

97
00:03:52,063 --> 00:03:54,403
At eight o'clock, Villeneuve orders his

98
00:03:54,403 --> 00:03:57,423
whole fleet to turn back toward Cádiz.

99
00:03:57,423 --> 00:03:58,273
In the light wind,

100
00:03:58,543 --> 00:03:59,713
the turn takes hours,

101
00:04:00,463 --> 00:04:02,793
and his line bends into a ragged curve.

102
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:05,030
The British form two columns.

103
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:07,630
Nelson leads one, in Victory.

104
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:09,490
His second in command,

105
00:04:09,780 --> 00:04:11,820
Cuthbert Collingwood, leads the other,

106
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:13,090
in the Royal Sovereign.

107
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:16,310
Nelson has 27 ships of the line,

108
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:18,120
more than 2,000 cannon,

109
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:19,990
and 17,000 men.

110
00:04:20,537 --> 00:04:21,647
At a quarter to noon,

111
00:04:21,937 --> 00:04:24,217
Nelson asks his signal officer to send a

112
00:04:24,217 --> 00:04:27,217
message: "England confides that every man

113
00:04:27,217 --> 00:04:29,857
will do his duty." The officer suggests

114
00:04:29,857 --> 00:04:33,347
one change. "Expects." It's quicker to signal.

115
00:04:33,977 --> 00:04:34,747
Nelson agrees.

116
00:04:35,590 --> 00:04:37,830
"England expects that every man will do

117
00:04:37,830 --> 00:04:40,890
his duty." Then one more signal: "Engage

118
00:04:40,890 --> 00:04:42,420
the enemy more closely."

119
00:04:43,356 --> 00:04:45,676
At noon, the first shot is fired at the

120
00:04:45,676 --> 00:04:48,696
Royal Sovereign. Collingwood turns to his

121
00:04:48,696 --> 00:04:52,216
officers. "Now, gentlemen, let us do something

122
00:04:52,216 --> 00:04:54,546
today which the world may talk of hereafter."

123
00:04:55,417 --> 00:04:57,687
Royal Sovereign, faster than the rest,

124
00:04:58,157 --> 00:05:00,207
smashes into the enemy line alone,

125
00:05:00,797 --> 00:05:02,997
and fires a broadside straight through the

126
00:05:02,997 --> 00:05:04,667
stern of a Spanish flagship.

127
00:05:05,383 --> 00:05:08,393
Behind her, HMS Belleisle is shot to pieces.

128
00:05:09,163 --> 00:05:10,273
Every mast is gone.

129
00:05:10,863 --> 00:05:13,403
She cannot steer. But she keeps her flag

130
00:05:13,403 --> 00:05:15,423
flying, for 45 minutes,

131
00:05:15,423 --> 00:05:16,533
until help arrives.

132
00:05:17,475 --> 00:05:19,755
To the north, Victory sails into the fire

133
00:05:19,755 --> 00:05:21,125
of four ships at once.

134
00:05:21,795 --> 00:05:23,825
For 40 minutes, she cannot fire back.

135
00:05:24,355 --> 00:05:25,685
Her wheel is shot away.

136
00:05:26,395 --> 00:05:27,885
Sailors steer her from below.

137
00:05:28,584 --> 00:05:30,924
Then she passes behind the French flagship,

138
00:05:30,924 --> 00:05:34,424
Bucentaure, and fires a broadside down the

139
00:05:34,424 --> 00:05:35,334
length of her decks.

140
00:05:36,006 --> 00:05:38,066
Victory locks together with another French

141
00:05:38,066 --> 00:05:41,246
ship, the Redoutable. Its crew gathers to

142
00:05:41,246 --> 00:05:41,616
board.

143
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:44,230
High in the Redoutable's rigging,

144
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:47,500
a French marksman is firing down onto Victory's

145
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:50,290
deck. At about a quarter past one,

146
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:52,990
a musket ball hits Nelson in the shoulder,

147
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:54,750
passes through his lung,

148
00:05:55,160 --> 00:05:56,530
and lodges in his spine.

149
00:05:57,243 --> 00:05:58,123
"They have done it at last,

150
00:05:58,123 --> 00:06:01,473
Hardy," he says. "My backbone is shot through."

151
00:06:01,803 --> 00:06:02,833
He is carried below.

152
00:06:03,692 --> 00:06:05,682
On deck, the French are about to board.

153
00:06:06,452 --> 00:06:09,952
Then HMS Temeraire comes alongside the Redoutable,

154
00:06:09,952 --> 00:06:13,294
and fires straight into the crowd of boarders.

155
00:06:13,564 --> 00:06:14,524
At five to two,

156
00:06:14,524 --> 00:06:18,524
the Redoutable surrenders. Of 643 men,

157
00:06:18,524 --> 00:06:20,674
99 can still fight.

158
00:06:21,416 --> 00:06:23,956
One by one, the enemy centre and rear are

159
00:06:23,956 --> 00:06:26,848
surrounded. The French flagship Bucentaure

160
00:06:26,848 --> 00:06:30,160
surrenders. Villeneuve is taken prisoner.

161
00:06:30,430 --> 00:06:33,090
The Santísima Trinidad, the most heavily

162
00:06:33,090 --> 00:06:34,750
armed warship in the world,

163
00:06:34,750 --> 00:06:38,700
with 130 guns, holds out for three hours,

164
00:06:39,230 --> 00:06:40,834
then strikes her colours.

165
00:06:41,104 --> 00:06:42,774
The Spanish fought bravely.

166
00:06:43,344 --> 00:06:43,984
Two of their captains,

167
00:06:43,984 --> 00:06:46,644
Churruca and Alcalá Galiano,

168
00:06:46,644 --> 00:06:48,074
were killed on their own decks.

169
00:06:48,924 --> 00:06:50,044
The front of the enemy line,

170
00:06:50,044 --> 00:06:51,284
which could have turned to help,

171
00:06:51,284 --> 00:06:52,884
fires a few shots,

172
00:06:52,884 --> 00:06:54,054
and sails away.

173
00:06:54,639 --> 00:06:55,969
Late in the afternoon,

174
00:06:56,299 --> 00:06:57,999
the French ship Achille catches fire,

175
00:06:57,999 --> 00:06:59,149
and explodes.

176
00:07:00,059 --> 00:07:02,109
Below decks, Nelson hears the cheering.

177
00:07:02,939 --> 00:07:04,769
Hardy tells him the victory is complete.

178
00:07:05,359 --> 00:07:08,069
"Thank God I have done my duty," he murmurs.

179
00:07:08,419 --> 00:07:09,589
At half past four,

180
00:07:09,619 --> 00:07:10,069
he dies.

181
00:07:11,173 --> 00:07:13,083
Nelson had ordered the fleet to anchor.

182
00:07:13,533 --> 00:07:14,683
A storm was coming.

183
00:07:15,073 --> 00:07:15,983
That night, it hit.

184
00:07:16,526 --> 00:07:18,746
For a week, a gale tore at the shattered

185
00:07:18,746 --> 00:07:21,746
ships. Many of the captured prizes sank

186
00:07:21,746 --> 00:07:23,656
or were driven onto the rocks.

187
00:07:24,346 --> 00:07:26,276
Some were retaken by their own prisoners.

188
00:07:27,186 --> 00:07:29,766
In the end, the British burned or sank several

189
00:07:29,766 --> 00:07:33,186
prizes themselves, even the giant Santísima

190
00:07:33,186 --> 00:07:33,636
Trinidad.

191
00:07:34,450 --> 00:07:36,450
Collingwood wrote that he would rather fight

192
00:07:36,450 --> 00:07:39,130
another battle than pass through such a

193
00:07:39,130 --> 00:07:39,840
week again.

194
00:07:40,603 --> 00:07:44,503
The count. Britain lost about 450 men killed,

195
00:07:44,503 --> 00:07:46,233
and not a single ship.

196
00:07:46,903 --> 00:07:49,443
The French and Spanish lost around 4,400

197
00:07:49,443 --> 00:07:53,213
killed, and 18 ships captured or destroyed.

198
00:07:54,303 --> 00:07:56,516
Only 11 made it back to Cádiz.

199
00:07:56,916 --> 00:07:59,396
Napoleon had won a great victory at Ulm

200
00:07:59,396 --> 00:08:02,176
the day before. He kept Trafalgar secret

201
00:08:02,176 --> 00:08:05,316
for weeks. When the news finally broke in

202
00:08:05,316 --> 00:08:07,876
Paris, the newspapers called it a great

203
00:08:07,876 --> 00:08:08,746
French victory.

204
00:08:09,551 --> 00:08:11,571
Villeneuve was released and went home to

205
00:08:11,571 --> 00:08:14,811
France. In 1806, he was found dead in an

206
00:08:14,811 --> 00:08:16,741
inn, stabbed six times.

207
00:08:17,191 --> 00:08:19,381
The official verdict: suicide.

208
00:08:19,984 --> 00:08:21,834
Trafalgar didn't end the war.

209
00:08:22,384 --> 00:08:24,804
Six weeks later, Napoleon won his greatest

210
00:08:24,804 --> 00:08:26,614
battle, at Austerlitz.

211
00:08:27,163 --> 00:08:28,953
But it settled who ruled the sea.

212
00:08:29,723 --> 00:08:30,993
For the rest of the century,

213
00:08:31,303 --> 00:08:33,523
no fleet seriously challenged the Royal

214
00:08:33,523 --> 00:08:34,093
Navy.

215
00:08:34,763 --> 00:08:35,883
And Nelson came home,

216
00:08:35,883 --> 00:08:37,463
in a barrel of brandy,

217
00:08:37,463 --> 00:08:40,213
to a hero's funeral at St Paul's.

218
00:08:40,732 --> 00:08:43,292
So, was Trafalgar won by Nelson's genius,

219
00:08:43,292 --> 00:08:45,302
or by Villeneuve's fear?

220
00:08:46,012 --> 00:08:46,772
Tell me in the comments,

221
00:08:46,772 --> 00:08:48,822
and subscribe to Buried Chronicles.

